      "description": "chris compose-ui-testing-patterns: interaction-state example launches interactionSource.emit(...) on a fresh detached TestScope() that is never advanced, so the emit never runs and the hover state is never applied before the assertion.",
      "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
      "reasoning": "Code at lines 97-105 verified verbatim: `TestScope().launch { interactionSource.emit(HoverInteraction.Enter()) }` then `composeTestRule.waitForIdle()`. TestScope() is backed by a StandardTestDispatcher/TestCoroutineScheduler which does NOT run queued coroutines eagerly (unlike UnconfinedTestDispatcher) — tasks only run when the scheduler is pumped via runCurrent()/advanceUntilIdle()/advanceTimeBy(). There is no runTest wrapping it and no manual advancement, so the launched coroutine body never executes and emit() never happens. composeTestRule.waitForIdle() synchronizes only Compose's own recomposition/idling, not an unrelated external scheduler. Therefore the comment 'Assert the visual/semantic change that hover produces' would be evaluated against the un-hovered state. This is the skill's flagship/unique technique, so teaching a non-working driver is material. (Ironically the skill correctly warns against LaunchedEffect at line 113 but substitutes an equally non-functional driver.) Severity major is fair."
